    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This essay won or received an honorable mention for The Boothe Prize for excellence in first-year writing. The Boothe Prize recognizes and rewards outstanding expository and argumentative writing by undergraduate students in first-year classes that satisfy the WR 1 requirement. In each award-winning essay, student writers demonstrate clarity of argument, excellent integration of research-based evidence, and compelling prose style.\r\nThis essay examines the way video game makers re-make games when they market them to distinct audiences, a process known as localization. Thomas Zhao looks carefully at various degrees of transformation that the makers of different video games choose. He demonstrates that the choices made in video game localization reflect not just on the practice of game design but on the relationship between the U.S. and Japan, and particularly American attitudes toward Japanese culture as a whole.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Zhao, R. (2024). Word for Word: Culture’s Impact on the Localization of Japanese Video Games. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/dr894ty2274. https://doi.org/10.25740/dr894ty2274.",
